WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.942 --> 00:00:06.442
(eerie music)

00:00:15.864 --> 00:00:17.174
- [Announcer] There's an expression we use

00:00:17.174 --> 00:00:20.174
for an unlikely or impossible event.

00:00:21.842 --> 00:00:25.012
When something has little or no chance,

00:00:25.012 --> 00:00:28.929
we say that it has a
snowball's chance in Hell.

00:00:30.263 --> 00:00:33.667
But those are the kinds of odds we like.

00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:37.384
In our experiment, we're going
to send a snowball to Hell.

00:00:37.384 --> 00:00:40.751
And try to bring it back safely.

00:00:40.751 --> 00:00:43.668
(intense drumming)

00:00:45.401 --> 00:00:47.659
We'll be attempting
this at an old foundry,

00:00:47.659 --> 00:00:49.424
where the molten metal
reaches temperatures

00:00:49.424 --> 00:00:51.257
of over 2,000 degrees.

00:00:52.201 --> 00:00:55.166
We think this makes it pretty
much to closest thing to Hell

00:00:55.166 --> 00:00:56.916
that exists on earth.

00:00:58.331 --> 00:01:01.390
To pull this off, we got
GE scientists and engineers

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:03.656
at the Global Research
Center to collaborate

00:01:03.656 --> 00:01:06.017
and come up with a solution.

00:01:06.017 --> 00:01:08.286
They drew on technology
from the power, aviation,

00:01:08.286 --> 00:01:10.578
and healthcare industries,
and developed a vessel

00:01:10.578 --> 00:01:12.983
made from a special super alloy.

00:01:12.983 --> 00:01:15.571
It was engineered to withstand
incredibly high temperatures,

00:01:15.571 --> 00:01:17.771
so we normally use it
to build jet engines,

00:01:17.771 --> 00:01:22.310
which have to endure extreme
environmental stresses.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:25.227
But it was never designed for this.

00:01:26.656 --> 00:01:28.726
We're putting the snowball
in this specially-designed

00:01:28.726 --> 00:01:33.456
3-D printed holder, and then
placing that in our vessel.

00:01:33.456 --> 00:01:36.123
(intense music)

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:40.931
Then we lower it into a vat

00:01:40.931 --> 00:01:44.681
and completely cover it
in molten metal slag.

00:01:50.385 --> 00:01:51.769
And when we pull it out,

00:01:51.769 --> 00:01:54.769
we hope our snowball will be intact.

00:01:55.914 --> 00:01:58.581
(intense music)

00:02:09.754 --> 00:02:12.587
(chains clinking)

00:02:16.063 --> 00:02:19.064
(machine whirring)

00:02:19.064 --> 00:02:21.314
(creaking)

00:02:34.992 --> 00:02:37.742
(fire crackling)

00:02:47.669 --> 00:02:50.502
(chains clinking)

00:02:56.334 --> 00:02:57.167
(victorious music)

00:02:57.167 --> 00:02:58.000
- Yeah!

00:03:04.283 --> 00:03:07.450
(inspirational music)

